  "worked_solution": "If $A=U\u03a3{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{\u03a3}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${\u03a3}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $\u03a3.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{\u03a3}^{+}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{\u03a3}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
